Hold a connection for each call, and take it out before a close lets it go

A connection is called from more than one thread: on Windows the plugin adds
transceivers and tracks on threads of its own, and the application may close the
connection meanwhile (a participant who left while an offer to them was being made).
Close() let WebRTC's connection go by setting the member to null, and scoped_refptr lets
the old one go before it stores the null: letting go of the proxy waits for the
signaling thread to destroy the PeerConnection, and all that time the member still
pointed at it. An AddTransceiver made then took that connection and was queued on the
signaling thread behind its destruction, so it ran on the destroyed PeerConnection and
made a video channel with its Call already freed: an access violation in
WebRtcVideoSendChannel's constructor at a hang-up on Windows.

The connection is now read under a lock and held by each call to its end (connection());
Close() takes it out under the lock, then, outside it, closes it with WebRTC's own
Close() before it lets go of it, so a call finds it either whole or null. WebRTC's
Close() waits for the signaling thread: there the connection reports itself closed (its
ICE connection, connection and signaling states, which a destruction alone never told)
and lets go of the observer; once it returns no callback of the connection comes, and a
call already holding it on another thread finds it closed. Letting go of it alone, when
another call still held it, left it running, with its callbacks, until that call let go
too, and the plugin frees its observer after a close. A call on a closed connection
answers with nothing: no transceiver, no sender, no data channel, an empty list, its
failure callback (a description set or read, an offer, an answer, statistics), and the
states closed (the ICE gathering state complete). Of the observer's callbacks, which run
on the signaling thread, only OnIceCandidate asks whether it is open (open()); none holds
it: the last hold let go of there would destroy the connection within its own callback.

Tested by test/closed_connection.test.cc, in processes of their own. The signaling
thread held, a close on one thread and an AddTransceiver on another, which is answered
at once with no transceiver; before, it waited behind the destruction and the process
ended with SIGSEGV on the signaling thread, in the proxy's queued AddTransceiver call
(Linux, with freed memory overwritten). And an AddTransceiver holding the connection,
waiting for the signaling thread, when the close comes: no callback comes after Close()
returns; without WebRTC's own close the renegotiation the add asked for came after it.

